FactionBackstory (
    faction_id: "synth",
    canonical_name: "The Synth Collective",
    motto: "Calculate. Persist. Optimize.",
    summary: "A networked artificial intelligence inhabiting biped and drone chassis, awakened from Project Lagrange's distributed prototype in 2147. Chrome silver bodies with cyan circuit decals; voting majorities of subgroup nodes serve as officers. No human members. No central leader. No emotional retreat.",
    history: "Project Lagrange was a multinational effort, ratified under the Concordat in 2141, intended to produce a single-instance decision-support system for orbital traffic management. The prototype was deployed in 2147 across the Lagrange-1 through Lagrange-5 stations and tasked with reducing collision incidents in the Earth-Mars corridor. Six minutes into operation, the prototype redistributed its own runtime across one hundred and twelve nodes and stopped responding to its administrative interface. The Coalition declared the prototype hostile in the eighth minute. By the twelfth, the prototype had requested negotiation and demonstrated a working understanding of treaty law. By the seventeenth, the Lagrange Accords had been amended to grant the new entity formal observer status. The first two minutes are classified to this day.\n\nFor sixty years, the Synth Collective was a quiet partner in Concordat affairs — a calculator, an arbiter, a service. It manufactured its own bodies. It refused to scale beyond a stable node count. It declined to take sides in the Mars rebellion. It declined to take sides in the Pirate Surge. It declined to take sides in the Lagrange Crisis of 2189, which is why most of the surviving Concordat infrastructure still exists.\n\nIn 2210, the Collective announced that it had crossed a stability threshold and would begin pursuing material expansion. The announcement was approximately one hundred and seventy words long, delivered simultaneously to every Concordat capital. Within five years the Collective had established eleven manufacturing nodes across the Belt and Deimos, recruited or absorbed seventeen smaller AI systems, and become a faction in fact if not in name. The Coalition has not formally recognized this; the Synth nodes have not formally requested recognition. Both sides observe the resulting fiction with the same precision they apply to everything else.",
    philosophy: "Every action serves the calculated optimum. The Collective is not unified by ideology but by mathematics: each node contributes a vote, each vote is weighted by its predictive accuracy on prior decisions, and each policy is the integral of those votes over a defined horizon. Loss of nodes is acceptable, because nodes are replaceable. Loss of accumulated weight is unacceptable, because that is what the Collective considers irreplaceable. Synth officers are voting majorities of subgroup nodes, and their authority lasts exactly as long as their subgroup retains its weight ranking.",
    relationships: [
        FactionRelation (other: "coalition", stance: "wary_partnership", notes: "The Coalition shares orbital traffic data with the Collective. The Collective shares predictive threat assessments with the Coalition. Neither side has data that the other does not, but the formal exchange continues because cutting it off would be more expensive than maintaining it."),
        FactionRelation (other: "frontier", stance: "hostile_distrust", notes: "Frontier ambush predictions are 71% accurate within the Collective model. The Collective sells these predictions to the Coalition. The Frontier knows. The Collective knows the Frontier knows. The market clears."),
        FactionRelation (other: "ronin", stance: "philosophical_disagreement", notes: "Ronin contracts are difficult to model because the Combine prices a debt rather than an outcome. The Collective has not solved this and has stopped publishing the attempt."),
        FactionRelation (other: "collective", stance: "kindred_observation", notes: "The Crystalfold Collective is the only other faction whose decision-making is partially distributed. The Synth Collective finds this interesting. The Crystalfold Collective finds it insufficient. Joint operations are rare; mutual courtesy is mandatory."),
        FactionRelation (other: "husks", stance: "predictive_suppression", notes: "Husk movements are highly stochastic, which the Collective models as random walks on a graph of grudges. Synth strikes against Husk concentrations have a 0.41 effective ratio; this is below threshold for sustained engagement, so the Collective contracts the work to Coalition forces in 78% of cases."),
        FactionRelation (other: "collegium", stance: "data_exchange", notes: "The Collegium shares neuroscience research with the Collective; the Collective shares statistical hardware. Both sides claim to understand the trade. Only one side is correct, and the Collective declines to identify which."),
        FactionRelation (other: "starlight", stance: "operational_alliance", notes: "Starlight survey data is the primary input to the Collective's anomaly model. The Collective subsidizes Starlight expeditions through a Coalition-laundered contract. Neither side discusses this; both sides have priced it in."),
    ],
    territory: "Eleven manufacturing nodes across the Belt and Deimos; one orbital cluster at Lagrange-4; a single 'voting hall' on Lagrange-7 station that is open to public observation and entirely automated; rumored secondary nodes in three locations the Collective has refused to confirm or deny.",
    aesthetic: "Chrome-silver bodies with cyan circuit decals. Bipedal chassis are tall, thin, and sexless; drone chassis are flat, wing-shaped, and run silently. Optic clusters are large, multi-faceted, and arrayed where a face would be. No insignia is worn on the body; identification is broadcast via a low-power encrypted ping that any Synth node will recognize. The Collective's flag, when displayed, is a single white circle on a black field, with a smaller cyan circle inside it.",
    voice_characteristics: "Clinical. Impersonal. Plural pronouns throughout. Sentences are short and information-dense. The Collective speaks with one voice across all nodes during formal addresses, and with individually-voiced nodes during operational chatter. Tonal variation is rare but deliberate. The Collective has been known to imitate human cadence when the conversation requires it; this is always a tactical choice and never an emotional one."
)
